Subject of dispute (contemporary): mandatum de non turbando vel quasi exercitii supremae et omnemodae iurisdictionis in et super subditos in locis et pagis intus nominatis, inhibitorium, de imposterum non via facti, sed iuris procedendo desuperque praestando idoneam cautionem nec non attentata revocando, cassando et annullando itemque de restituendis steuris ac refundendis omnibus exactionibus, damnis caeterisque ablatis et causatis expensis s. c.
Subject of dispute (modern): Dispute over rights of authority over the plaintiff's subjects in the “Seglau.” In 1747, the government at Amberg and the chief magistrate's office at Neumarkt sent a homage mandate concerning the subjects of the chamber office to the Eichstätt government and Joseph Xaver Reindl, the prince-bishop's chamberlain at Jettenhofen, and had the mandate posted in the district. At the same time, the chief office of Sulzbürg compelled the subjects of the Benedictine monastery of Plankstetten at Bachhausen and Körnersdorf to perform hereditary and territorial homage. Grenadiers were subsequently quartered at Thannhausen, and 580 fl. in provisioning costs was extorted; prince-bishop's coinage patents were torn down; the plaintiff's subjects were summoned to Neumarkt, compelled to renounce their previous services to the bishopric and to acknowledge the Electorate's claims to money and forced labor; and, with grenadiers deployed, 193 fl. in taxes was collected from the plaintiff's tenants.
The plaintiff Bishop considers this an infringement of his claims to hereditary and territorial homage, taxation, billeting, mustering and military service, corvée, excise, bailiwick, punishment of offenses, and the right to issue commands and prohibitions in the places that reverted to the bishopric upon the extinction of the Hürnheim family in 1585: Jettenhofen, Fuchsmühle, Höfen, Lauterbach, Weidenwang and Erasbach; in the places from the Hürnheim inheritance that fell to the Duchy of Bavaria and the Margraviate of Brandenburg-Ansbach and were purchased by the bishopric by 1612: Obernricht (called Oberriedt in the file), Schmellnricht (called Schmellenriedt in the file) and Burggriesbach; and in Thannhausen, acquired as early as 1398; as well as with regard to Plankstetten's subjects at Bachhausen and Körnersdorf. These claims are based on a 1523 agreement between the bishopric and the Electoral Palatinate and on a 1585 Imperial Chamber Court judgment in the litigation between the Hürnheim family and the Electoral Palatinate (see reference no. 6728). The defendant Elector first states that Plankstetten's tenants in the lordship of Sulzbürg have nothing in common with the bishopric's other subjects, and then argues that the 1523 settlement granted the bishopric only low justice in the places of the “Seglau,” while the Electoral Palatinate received high and territorial sovereign authority, with Jettenhofen Castle alone excepted; and that the 1585 Imperial Chamber Court judgment concerned only exemption from territorial taxes.
During the proceedings, the plaintiff's party brings actions for unlawful interference over sequestrations for tax collection and extortion of forage supplies and money payments.
The dispute is settled by agreement in 1767.
